finding: Parity still admits wrong imported scenes, and the claimed position bound is not fully derived. 1. Defect: examples/gltf/tests/parity.ts:122-140 checks spawned entities only through Transform, then reads the material through model.primitives[i].material, not the entity's MeshMaterial; geometry likewise comes from primitive.mesh (line 76), not the spawned MeshInstance. A wrong spawn binding (e.g. every entity gets the first material, or later primitives get the first mesh) leaves this parity green. placement.node.ts only proves component presence for the truck and the first BoxTextured mesh, not the truck's per-entity bindings. Compare the entity bindings as well. 2. Defect: examples/gltf/tests/parity.ts:21-24,122-138 uses an unexplained absolute 1e-5 for both model and spawned matrices. An erroneous 5e-6 translation at an authored zero passes, even though zero and that translation are distinct representable float32 values; the position half-step allowance does not justify transform slack. The claimed matrix mutation reds show sensitivity to the chosen perturbations, not equality or a derived arithmetic error bound. 3. Defect: examples/gltf/tests/parity.ts:34-38 omits rounding code/65535 to float32 from its decode bound. The error of that operation must be propagated through extent, in addition to product and sum rounding. A concrete correctly nearest-quantized float32 input: code=257, extent=fround(1.1)=1.100000023841858, offset=-0.004313725978136063, expected=fround(-0.000008392856667586846)=-0.000008392856216232758. Its normalized input encodes to 257 (also with the float32 pack arithmetic), the implemented decode is zero, error=0.000008392856216232758, but the current bound=0.000008392695055728401. This can false-red correct data; it is not the claimed upper bound. No free epsilon is needed to account for the missing operation. Independently examined: unit/settled plan/example and physics rules; both commit messages; generator, parity, mesh quantizer/decoder, importer spawn, example app/scenes and placement tests. Explicit example *.node.ts/*.test.ts: 24 pass, 0 fail (159959 assertions), real Apple M4 Max Metal. Literal `bun test ./examples/gltf/...` matches nothing on this Bun, so files were enumerated. Requested three.js dependency/import rg: no matches. Pinned generator regeneration was byte-identical, SHA-256 b349dc43e6977b08bd4a07d18e1f4c62f14bb85da5e1b7a55682f9e7e58a3620, with no worktree diff. Full arrays are the tool's declared example format, named to clauses, and earn their size through elementwise packed-buffer comparison (including unreferenced vertices), not digest-only comparisons. Independent raw-buffer/accessor/material/sampler audit passed for TextureSettingsTest and SimpleMeshes; additionally decoded TriangleWithoutIndices and independently computed its implicit +Z flat normals, sequential indices and default material, against the pinned specification. Authored texture indices are retained rather than three.js's shared-cache associations. No example-shape rule violation found; look remains the person's. Notes/uncertainty: implicitNormals is record metadata rather than separately compared; unlit lighting fields and non-MASK cutoff are intentionally absent/irrelevant. Material float32 comparison and exact local index order hold. Mutation claims each map to an assertion on the relevant non-default/core fixture, but their historical runs were not independently replayed (no source edits); they do not cover the holes above. Browser/look and full pre-land checks were not rerun in this review.
